=base:summary.cmark

A `corporate` layout example: a fictional marketing page for a "Budgetz
Cloud" product, showing the hero, feature grid, pricing table, call to
action, and testimonial strip that the layout is meant for.

=base:main-content.cmark

<div class="hero">
  <h1>Budgetz Cloud</h1>
  <p class="hero-subtitle">Track team spend in real time, without the
  spreadsheet gymnastics. Set up in an afternoon, keep everyone honest for
  years.</p>
  <div class="hero-actions">
    <a class="button" href="#pricing">See pricing</a>
    <a class="button secondary" href="#contact">Talk to sales</a>
  </div>
</div>

=base:main-content.cmark

## Why teams switch to Budgetz Cloud

<div class="feature-grid">
  <div class="feature-card">
    <h3>Live budgets</h3>
    <p>Every team sees its own bucket update as spend happens, not at
    month-end reconciliation.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature-card">
    <h3>Delegated keys</h3>
    <p>Hand a scoped key to a sub-team or a robot; reclaim what it does not
    use.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature-card">
    <h3>Audit trail</h3>
    <p>Every consumption and replenishment is timestamped and attributable,
    no export required.</p>
  </div>
</div>

=base:pricing.json
{"pricingFeatures": ["Buckets", "Delegated keys", "Support"]
,"pricingPlans":
  [ {"name": "Starter", "price": "$0", "values": ["3", "1", "Community"]}
  , {"name": "Team", "price": "$29/mo", "values": ["25", "20", "Priority"]}
  , {"name": "Scale", "price": "Talk to us", "values": ["Unlimited", "Unlimited", "Dedicated"]}
  ]
}

=base:main-content.cmark

## What our early customers say

<div class="testimonial-strip">
  <div class="testimonial">
    We went from a shared spreadsheet nobody trusted to a dashboard every
    team lead checks before standup.
    <span class="testimonial-author">Head of Platform, mid-size fintech</span>
  </div>
  <div class="testimonial">
    Delegated keys are the feature that sold the rest of the team: our
    robots finally have their own budget instead of one shared secret.
    <span class="testimonial-author">Staff engineer, dev tooling team</span>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="cta-banner" id="contact">
  <h2>Ready to see it on your own numbers?</h2>
  <p>No credit card. Import a CSV of last month's spend and get a live
  dashboard in minutes.</p>
  <a class="button" href="mailto:hello@example.invalid">Get started</a>
</div>

<p>&#8203;</p>

This page uses the <code>corporate</code> article layout: set
<code>{"layout":"corporate"}</code> in <code>base:build-info.json</code> and
import <code>/css/corporate.css</code> next to the usual stylesheets. Unlike
the <code>article</code> layout, it renders no byline, no publication date,
and no topic links or glossary — only the site-wide menu and footer plus
whatever <code>main-content</code>, <code>callout</code>, and
<code>pricing</code> sections the page declares, each as its own full-width
section. See [layouts](/layouts.html) for the other article layouts.
